From: Caboose <caboose@local>
Subject: [PATCH 93/94] Fill invalidated coherent memory on first use, and read
 back every copy into it

Copies into buffers the GPU also reads (vertex, index, uniform, storage, indirect or
transfer-source usage) were never marked GPU-written, so they were never read back: DXVK
creates D3D11 staging buffers with that usage, and d3d11_stress read 0 from every
readback. Every copy is now marked; copies into such buffers stay out of completion-record
captures.

The invalidate after a wait, and an application's, now leave the blocks of watched coherent
memory that can wait lazy (caboose-bounded-memory/write_watch.h): their pages are dropped,
and the first access to one, by the application or the kernel, is filled from the phone by
the write watch's filler, three 16 KiB blocks to a round trip, write-protected so later
writes are recorded as before. The fill reads on a connection of the process's own beside
the device stream (an auxiliary stream: SocketStream.cpp), so it takes no lock a faulting
thread may hold, the encoder's included. Since that connection is not ordered with the
device stream, a block is lazy only when the GPU writes the invalidate covers have
completed and the last bytes the device stream sent for it went before a completed
submission. Blocks holding unsent writes, outside the mapping, or of memory a completion
record is being stored into are read at once, as is everything without the completion
feed, without userfaultfd or with CABOOSE_LAZY_READBACK=0. A flush fills the lazy blocks
it sends, an unmap fills the memory's, and a completion record stores only the parts that
are not lazy.

A fill always returns. A device of an executor the process no longer has is not asked (the
fill installs zeros), an executor's loss shuts the auxiliary stream, whose read never waits
for a new executor, and a new executor gets a new one, so no fault waits on a gone executor.

Copies into buffers the GPU also reads no longer keep a batch from being speculated: the
invalidate after a speculated wait takes its writes as completed ones, making them lazy with
the submission to wait for (a fill reads them once its record arrives) or reading them on the
device stream behind the WAIT_SERIAL the encoder sends first. Only writes whose bytes the
record carries still hold a batch to its record.
